      "correspondence": "Integrality of h^{-1} and h^{-1}g expresses Λ₀⊆H and Λ⊆H respectively. In Smith coordinates the element u used by the proof represents Λ₀+Λ, with valuation min(d_i,0) in coordinate i. The inverse-integral coset series at X² with trivial character counts superlattices of uΛ₀. The dual sublattice calculation in the text is equivalent.",
      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "review": "An independent reader checked the final text against the common-superlattice and Hermite-form proofs, the twice-counted length, and the disk of cancellation. The numerical fiber was also recomputed by enumerating all order-two and order-four subgroups of (Z/4)^2. Coefficients 1,3,7 cancel to 1,0,0 against 1−3T+2T²."
    },
    {
      "id": "SPE-02",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "title": "A triangular spherical function gives both linear Euler products",
      "anchor": "lifts-split-borel",
      "statement": "A normalized zonal coefficient with Satake parameters α_i admits a right-K-invariant Borel function having the same finite sum on each Cartan double coset and diagonal parameters γ_i=α_i q^{i−(a−1)/2}. The integral and inverse-integral coset sums supply the reciprocal factors with ratios q^{a−1−i}γ_i X and q^i γ_i^{-1}X. Removing the intermediate lattice multiplicity gives the GL doubling identity. Substituting X=q^{−(a/2+s)} yields the local factors α_i^{±1}q^{−(s+1/2)}.",
      "hypotheses": "The GL doubling theorem is for a characteristic-zero nonarchimedean local field, normalized Haar volume vol(GL_a(O))=1, a representation with the specified Satake data, and a zonal coefficient normalized by ω(1)=1. The intermediate two-variable sums are first evaluated for sufficiently small |X|. The twisted application takes α_i=zβ_i.",
      "definitions": [
        "normalized zonal coefficient",
        "upper triangular Borel subgroup",
        "Satake parameters in the normalized convention",
        "inverse transpose with basis reversal"
      ],
